{
 "lead": "A Fourier transform turns a time waveform into a frequency recipe: how much of each frequency the signal contains. Filters, antennas and cables act on frequency, so communication problems are easier there. A band-pass signal has all its content in a narrow band around a carrier, as an FM station near 100 MHz does. This chapter reviews the transform, its pairs and properties, and the low-pass description of band-pass signals.",
 "prereq": "You need: complex exponentials e<sup>jθ</sup> = cos θ + j sin θ and integration; the transform is taught in full with signals and systems.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The transform and its inverse",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The Fourier transform writes a signal x(t) as a sum of complex exponentials e<sup>j2πft</sup>, each with a complex weight X(f); the inverse builds x(t) back."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "X(f) = ∫ x(t) e<sup>−j2πft</sup> dt",
     "where": "the integral runs over all t from −∞ to ∞; X(f) is in volts per hertz (V/Hz) when x(t) is in volts; f is frequency in hertz; j = √−1."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "x(t) = ∫ X(f) e<sup>j2πft</sup> df",
     "where": "the integral runs over all f; it adds every frequency component back into the time signal."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "|X(f)| is the magnitude spectrum and ∠X(f) the phase spectrum. The unit V/Hz shows that X(f) is a density: only an integral over a band gives volts. Finite-energy signals always have a transform; power signals (cos, a constant) are handled with impulses δ(f)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Standard pairs and the properties used in every problem",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Define sinc(u) = sin(πu)/(πu), with sinc(0) = 1 and zeros at u = ±1, ±2, …. The pairs below (a, A &gt; 0) cover most examination questions."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "x(t)",
      "X(f)"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "rectangular pulse, height A for |t| &lt; T/2",
       "A T sinc(fT)"
      ],
      [
       "2AW sinc(2Wt)",
       "A for |f| &lt; W, 0 outside"
      ],
      [
       "δ(t)",
       "1"
      ],
      [
       "1 (a constant)",
       "δ(f)"
      ],
      [
       "cos(2πf<sub>0</sub>t)",
       "½[δ(f − f<sub>0</sub>) + δ(f + f<sub>0</sub>)]"
      ],
      [
       "sin(2πf<sub>0</sub>t)",
       "(1/2j)[δ(f − f<sub>0</sub>) − δ(f + f<sub>0</sub>)]"
      ],
      [
       "e<sup>−at</sup> u(t)",
       "1/(a + j2πf)"
      ],
      [
       "e<sup>−a|t|</sup>",
       "2a/(a² + 4π²f²)"
      ],
      [
       "e<sup>−πt²</sup>",
       "e<sup>−πf²</sup>"
      ],
      [
       "sgn(t)",
       "1/(jπf)"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "<b>Linearity:</b> a x<sub>1</sub>(t) + b x<sub>2</sub>(t) ↔ a X<sub>1</sub>(f) + b X<sub>2</sub>(f).",
      "<b>Time shift:</b> x(t − t<sub>0</sub>) ↔ X(f) e<sup>−j2πft<sub>0</sub></sup>. The magnitude is unchanged; only the phase changes.",
      "<b>Frequency shift:</b> x(t) e<sup>j2πf<sub>c</sub>t</sup> ↔ X(f − f<sub>c</sub>), a move to +f<sub>c</sub> only. A real carrier, cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t) = ½(e<sup>j2πf<sub>c</sub>t</sup> + e<sup>−j2πf<sub>c</sub>t</sup>), gives the <b>modulation theorem</b>: x(t) cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t) ↔ ½X(f − f<sub>c</sub>) + ½X(f + f<sub>c</sub>).",
      "<b>Convolution:</b> x(t) * h(t) ↔ X(f) H(f), and multiplication in time is convolution in frequency.",
      "<b>Scaling:</b> x(at) ↔ (1/|a|) X(f/a). A shorter pulse has a wider spectrum.",
      "<b>Duality:</b> if x(t) ↔ X(f), then X(t) ↔ x(−f): hence rectangle and sinc on both sides of the table.",
      "<b>Parseval (Rayleigh energy theorem):</b> ∫ |x(t)|² dt = ∫ |X(f)|² df, both in V²·s (energy per ohm); |X(f)|² is the energy spectral density."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "LTI systems and distortionless transmission",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A linear time-invariant (LTI) system with impulse response h(t) gives y(t) = x(t) * h(t), so Y(f) = X(f) H(f), where H(f) is the frequency response."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Write H(f) = |H(f)| e<sup>jθ(f)</sup>. A system is <b>distortionless</b> when every frequency gets the same gain K and the same time delay t<sub>d</sub>: y(t) = K x(t − t<sub>d</sub>). In frequency this is H(f) = K e<sup>−j2πft<sub>d</sub></sup>. The gain is flat, and the phase θ(f) = −2πft<sub>d</sub> is a straight line through the origin: the phase lag in radians grows with frequency, while the delay stays the same for every frequency."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Phase delay τ<sub>p</sub> = −θ(f)/(2πf) is the delay of one frequency; group delay τ<sub>g</sub> = −(1/2π) dθ/df is the delay of the envelope (θ in radians). With linear phase both equal t<sub>d</sub>; otherwise a pulse spreads even when |H(f)| is flat."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Band-pass signals and the low-pass equivalent",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A <b>band-pass signal</b> has a spectrum that is zero outside a band of width B centred at +f<sub>c</sub> and at −f<sub>c</sub>, with f<sub>c</sub> much larger than B. Its positive-frequency content lies between f<sub>c</sub> − B/2 and f<sub>c</sub> + B/2. Any such signal can be written in <b>canonical form</b> with two low-pass signals:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>x(t) = x<sub>I</sub>(t) cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t) − x<sub>Q</sub>(t) sin(2πf<sub>c</sub>t)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here x<sub>I</sub>(t) (in-phase) and x<sub>Q</sub>(t) (quadrature) are real low-pass signals of bandwidth B/2; the pair, the <b>low-pass equivalent</b>, carries everything in x(t). Each can be sampled at B samples per second, 2B in total, far below the more than 2(f<sub>c</sub> + B/2) per second the band-pass signal itself would need. <a href=\"#hilbert-transform-and-complex-envelope\">Hilbert transform and complex envelope</a> shows how to find x<sub>I</sub> and x<sub>Q</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Name the bandwidth. A baseband spectrum is measured on positive frequencies: a pulse of width T has first-null bandwidth 1/T and 3-dB bandwidth 0.443/T. After modulation both sides sit around f<sub>c</sub>, so the band-pass values double: 2/T null to null and 0.886/T between half-power points."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A rectangular pulse is x(t) = 2 V for |t| &lt; 0.5 ms and 0 elsewhere. (a) Find X(f) and X(0). (b) Find the first-null and the 3-dB bandwidths. (c) Find the pulse energy (per ohm) and the share of it inside the main lobe |f| &lt; 1/T.",
   "steps": [
    "Width T = 1 ms = 1×10<sup>−3</sup> s and height A = 2 V.",
    "From the table, X(f) = A T sinc(fT). A T = 2 V × 1×10<sup>−3</sup> s = 2×10<sup>−3</sup> V/Hz, so X(f) = 2×10<sup>−3</sup> sinc(10<sup>−3</sup>f) V/Hz and X(0) = 2 mV/Hz.",
    "The first null is at fT = 1, so f = 1/T = 1 kHz: the first-null bandwidth is 1 kHz.",
    "Half power means |X(f)| = X(0)/√2, that is sinc(fT) = 0.707. Solving gives fT = 0.443, so the 3-dB bandwidth is 0.443/T = 443 Hz.",
    "Energy in time: E = A²T = (2 V)² × 1×10<sup>−3</sup> s = 4×10<sup>−3</sup> V²·s.",
    "Main lobe (Parseval): ∫ |X(f)|² df over ±1 kHz = A²T ∫ sinc²(u) du over ±1 = 4×10<sup>−3</sup> × 0.903 = 3.61×10<sup>−3</sup> V²·s, 90.3 % of the energy."
   ],
   "answer": "X(f) = 2×10<sup>−3</sup> sinc(10<sup>−3</sup>f) V/Hz with X(0) = 2 mV/Hz; first-null bandwidth 1 kHz and 3-dB bandwidth 443 Hz; energy 4×10<sup>−3</sup> V²·s, of which 90.3 % lies in the main lobe."
  },
  {
   "question": "The same pulse modulates a 10 kHz carrier: y(t) = x(t) cos(2π×10<sup>4</sup>t). Find Y(f), its peak value, the band it occupies (null to null), its energy and its in-phase and quadrature components about f<sub>c</sub> = 10 kHz.",
   "steps": [
    "Modulation theorem: Y(f) = ½X(f − 10<sup>4</sup>) + ½X(f + 10<sup>4</sup>) = 10<sup>−3</sup> sinc(10<sup>−3</sup>(f − 10<sup>4</sup>)) + 10<sup>−3</sup> sinc(10<sup>−3</sup>(f + 10<sup>4</sup>)) V/Hz.",
    "Each copy peaks at half of 2 mV/Hz, that is 1 mV/Hz, at f = ±10 kHz.",
    "The positive copy has its first nulls at 10 kHz ± 1 kHz, so it occupies 9 kHz to 11 kHz: the null-to-null bandwidth is 2 kHz, twice the baseband first-null bandwidth.",
    "Energy: two copies, each of half height, so E<sub>y</sub> = 2 × ¼ × 4×10<sup>−3</sup> = 2×10<sup>−3</sup> V²·s; in time, cos² averages ½, the same value.",
    "Canonical form: y(t) = x(t) cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t), so x<sub>I</sub>(t) = x(t), the 2 V, 1 ms pulse, and x<sub>Q</sub>(t) = 0; its 1 kHz first-null bandwidth is half the 2 kHz band."
   ],
   "answer": "Y(f) is two half-height sinc copies, peak 1 mV/Hz at ±10 kHz, occupying 9 to 11 kHz (2 kHz null to null); energy 2×10<sup>−3</sup> V²·s; x<sub>I</sub>(t) = x(t) and x<sub>Q</sub>(t) = 0."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Bandwidth is not one number: say first-null, 3-dB or null-to-null, and baseband (1/T for the pulse) or band-pass (2/T).",
  "x(t) cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t) gives two copies of half height, ½X(f ∓ f<sub>c</sub>). Writing X(f − f<sub>c</sub>) alone is the shift by e<sup>j2πf<sub>c</sub>t</sup>, not by a real carrier.",
  "A time shift multiplies X(f) by e<sup>−j2πft<sub>0</sub></sup>; |X(f)| does not change.",
  "Distortionless means constant delay for every frequency. It is the phase, θ(f) = −2πft<sub>d</sub>, that grows with f. Put θ in radians in τ<sub>p</sub> and τ<sub>g</sub>; degrees give an answer wrong by 180/π.",
  "Time convolution pairs with frequency multiplication. Y(f) = X(f)/H(f) is undoing a filter (equalization), not passing a signal through it."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the Fourier transform pair for a rectangular pulse of height A and width T.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "x(t) = A for |t| &lt; T/2 and 0 elsewhere; X(f) = A T sinc(fT), with sinc(u) = sin(πu)/(πu)."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does a time shift not change the magnitude spectrum?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The shift multiplies X(f) by e<sup>−j2πft<sub>0</sub></sup>, whose magnitude is 1 at every f. So |X(f)| stays the same and only the phase changes, by −2πft<sub>0</sub>."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A pulse is x(t) = 4 V for |t| &lt; 0.1 ms and 0 elsewhere. Find X(0), the first-null bandwidth and the null-to-null bandwidth after it modulates a carrier.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "T = 0.2 ms. X(0) = A T = 4 × 2×10<sup>−4</sup> = 8×10<sup>−4</sup> V/Hz. First null at 1/T = 5 kHz. On a carrier the band runs from f<sub>c</sub> − 5 kHz to f<sub>c</sub> + 5 kHz: 10 kHz null to null."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "An LTI system has H(f) = 5 e<sup>−j2πf(0.2 ms)</sup>. Find the output for input x(t) and say whether the transmission is distortionless.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "|H(f)| = 5 is flat and θ(f) = −2πf × 0.2 ms is linear, so y(t) = 5 x(t − 0.2 ms): distortionless, with phase delay and group delay both 0.2 ms."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A band-pass signal has f<sub>c</sub> = 100 kHz and bandwidth B = 10 kHz. Give its band edges and the bandwidth of each low-pass component.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Lower edge f<sub>c</sub> − B/2 = 95 kHz; upper edge f<sub>c</sub> + B/2 = 105 kHz. x<sub>I</sub> and x<sub>Q</sub> each have bandwidth B/2 = 5 kHz."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Every software-defined radio stores signals as I and Q samples, x<sub>I</sub> and x<sub>Q</sub> sampled at a low rate, and every spectrum analyser computes spectra with the fast Fourier transform. Search for complex baseband and group delay distortion.",
 "figure": {
